\ cpt16.4 - make the Forth compiler emit CPT16 tokens instead of cells.
\
\ Same trick as cv8.4, one encoding earlier. Until this file existed
\ every 16-bit-token image was TRANSLATED from a cell image by
\ tools/layout.py, so it could RUN everything compiled into it but could
\ not compile a new definition: `,` and `COMPILE,` lay down cells, and
\ the engine then reads those cells as tokens. `: SQ DUP * ;` did not
\ even crash - it quietly computed the wrong answer.
\
\ HOW IT IS USED. Every word here is named `X16` because redefining `;`
\ in the CELL image would break the cell compiler on the very next
\ definition. Load this, dump the dictionary, and translate with
\ `--compiler-overlay 16`, which swaps each `X16` body into `X`. The
\ overlay's own bodies are compiled by the cell compiler and translated
\ like any other word; what changes is that the RESULTING image's
\ compiler emits tokens.
\
\ WHY THIS IS EASIER THAN SOD16. A CPT16 call needs no word number and
\ no table lookup: the target is `base + (v << shift)`, so the compiler
\ can compute a call token from an address with a subtract and a shift.
\ SOD16's table is built once at load and has no room to grow, which is
\ why it needs an escape hatch and this does not. That difference is the
\ main argument for CPT16 and it only becomes visible here, at the point
\ where the system has to compile for itself.

\ ---- opcode numbers -------------------------------------------------
\ The primitive band is kernel.4's PRIMITIVE order, so these are small
\ fixed integers rather than the cell image's code-field addresses.
  0 CONSTANT NOOP-OP
  1 CONSTANT EXIT-OP
  2 CONSTANT LIT16-OP
  3 CONSTANT BRANCH-OP
  4 CONSTANT 0BRANCH-OP
 68 CONSTANT LIT32-OP
 69 CONSTANT DOVAR-OP
 70 CONSTANT DODOES-OP
 71 CONSTANT LIT64-OP

\ ---- raw emission ---------------------------------------------------
: OP, ( n --- )
\ Append one 16-bit token, little-endian. Every operation in this
\ encoding is a whole number of these.
  DUP 255 AND C, 8 RSHIFT 255 AND C, ;

: W@ ( a-addr --- n )
\ Fetch one 16-bit token.
  DUP C@ SWAP 1+ C@ 8 LSHIFT OR ;

: !W ( n a-addr --- )
\ Store one 16-bit token.
  2DUP C! 1+ SWAP 8 RSHIFT 255 AND SWAP C! ;

\ ---- the call shift -------------------------------------------------
\ A call token is 256 + ((target - START) >> SHIFT). SHIFT is a build
\ option of the engine and the translator (1 for the plain CPT16 stage,
\ 2 or 3 once calls are cell-scaled), and this file is compiled long
\ before either is chosen. So it is MEASURED, once, from a probe planted
\ here for the purpose.
\
\ The probe may not mention any address. `[']` looked like the obvious
\ way to find it and is wrong: it compiles an absolute xt of the CELL
\ session, which the translator does not relocate, so the first version
\ of this read a 64-bit literal pointing into a process that had already
\ exited. Everything below is relative instead.

: CPT-ANCHOR ( --- a-addr )
\ The return address of whoever called this, i.e. the address of the
\ token immediately after that call. A word can therefore find out where
\ its own body is without anything having written the address down.
  R@ ;

: CPT-PROBE ( --- a-addr )
\ Returns the address of its own second token, and its body contains a
\ call to ITSELF - a call whose target is therefore known without any
\ address having been written down.
\
\ The self-call sits inside an `IF` that never fires rather than after
\ an `EXIT`, because the translator stops reading a body at its first
\ EXIT: the first version put the RECURSE in the tail and it was
\ silently dropped, leaving two NOOPs where the call should have been.
  CPT-ANCHOR DUP 0= IF RECURSE THEN ;

VARIABLE CPT-SHIFT-V ( --- a-addr )
-1 CPT-SHIFT-V !

: CPT-HAS? ( body want --- body flag )
\ Whether token `want` appears among the first 12 tokens of `body`. The
\ self-call's exact position depends on how the kernel compiles IF, so
\ it is searched for rather than indexed - that keeps this working if
\ the surrounding code is ever rearranged.
  SWAP 12 0 DO
    2DUP I 2 * + W@ =
    IF SWAP DROP -1 UNLOOP EXIT THEN
  LOOP SWAP DROP 0 ;

: CPT-SHIFT ( --- n )
\ The call scale shift, measured once and cached. The probe's body
\ address is known from the anchor, and its self-call encodes that same
\ address shifted, so the only unknown is the shift.
  CPT-SHIFT-V @ DUP 0< 0= IF EXIT THEN DROP
  CPT-PROBE 2 -                          ( body )
  8 0 DO
    DUP START @ - I RSHIFT 256 +         ( body want )
    CPT-HAS? IF
      DROP I DUP CPT-SHIFT-V ! UNLOOP EXIT
    THEN
  LOOP
  DROP 0 DUP CPT-SHIFT-V ! ;

\ ---- literals -------------------------------------------------------
: LIT, ( n --- )
\ Append a literal in the narrowest form that holds it.
\
\ The LIT64 arm is not decoration. Before it existed the translator
\ masked every literal to 32 bits, so a wider one was silently
\ truncated; no image in this encoding had ever compiled a literal, so
\ nothing had ever exercised it. The CORE suite's MAX-INT on an 8-byte
\ cell is the first value that does.
  DUP 65536 U< IF LIT16-OP OP, OP, EXIT THEN
  \ Fits a SIGNED 32-bit field? n + 2^31 < 2^32, which also rejects the
  \ wide negatives without a separate test.
  1 CELLS 4 = IF LIT32-OP OP, DUP OP, 16 RSHIFT OP, EXIT THEN
  DUP 2147483648 + 4294967296 U< IF
    LIT32-OP OP, DUP OP, 16 RSHIFT OP, EXIT
  THEN
  LIT64-OP OP,
  DUP OP, DUP 16 RSHIFT OP, DUP 32 RSHIFT OP, 48 RSHIFT OP, ;

: LITERAL16 ( n --- )
  LIT, ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- calls ----------------------------------------------------------
: CALL, ( a-addr --- )
\ Append a call to the body at a-addr. One token, no table, no word
\ number: the whole of CPT16's call mechanism is this line.
  START @ - CPT-SHIFT RSHIFT 256 + OP, ;

: COMPILE,16 ( xt --- )
\ Add the execution semantics of xt to the current definition: a
\ primitive becomes its opcode token, anything else a call.
  DUP >NAME C@ 32 AND IF
    \ A primitive's translated body is one token holding its opcode -
    \ W@, not @: the cell compiler read a whole cell here because a
    \ token WAS a cell.
    W@ OP,
  ELSE
    CALL,
  THEN ;

: ;16 ( --- )
  EXIT-OP OP, 0 STATE ! ?CSP REVEAL ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- control flow ---------------------------------------------------
\ A branch operand is a signed 16-bit count of TOKENS, measured from the
\ operand itself: the engine does `ip += 2 * (int16_t)TOK(ip)` with ip
\ still pointing at the operand.

: >MARK ( --- a-addr )
  HERE 0 OP, ;

: >RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
  DUP HERE SWAP - 2 / SWAP !W ;

: <RESOLVE ( a-addr --- )
  HERE - 2 / OP, ;

: BEGIN16 ( --- a-addr )
  HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: UNTIL16 ( a-addr --- )
  0BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: AGAIN16 ( a-addr --- )
  BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: IF16 ( --- a-addr )
  0BRANCH-OP OP, >MARK ; IMMEDIATE

: THEN16 ( a-addr --- )
  >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: ELSE16 ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2 )
  BRANCH-OP OP, >MARK SWAP >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

: WHILE16 ( a-addr1 --- a-addr2 a-addr1 )
  POSTPONE IF16 SWAP ; IMMEDIATE

: REPEAT16 ( a-addr1 a-addr2 --- )
  BRANCH-OP OP, <RESOLVE >RESOLVE ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- defining words -------------------------------------------------
: CREATE16 ( "ccc" --- )
\ A data body is [DOVAR][pad to a cell][parameter field]: DOVAR pushes
\ the next CELL-ALIGNED address, so the pad is never executed.
  HEADER REVEAL DOVAR-OP OP, ALIGN ;

: VARIABLE16 ( "ccc" --- )
  CREATE16 0 , ;

: CONSTANT16 ( x "ccc" --- )
  HEADER REVEAL LIT, EXIT-OP OP, ;

: RECURSE16 ( --- )
  LAST @ NAME> CALL, ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- DO ... LOOP ----------------------------------------------------
\ (LOOP) and (+LOOP) read an inline CELL operand holding a BYTE offset,
\ and it must stay cell-aligned - the one place this encoding keeps cell
\ granularity. The translator pads BEFORE the call so the operand lands
\ aligned just after it; the runtime compiler does the same. The pad is
\ NOOPs, and they do execute, once per loop.

: OPERAND-ALIGN ( --- )
\ Emit NOOPs until a one-token call would leave HERE cell-aligned.
  BEGIN HERE 2 + 1 CELLS 1- AND WHILE NOOP-OP OP, REPEAT ;

: DO16 ( --- a-addr )
  POSTPONE (DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 0 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: LOOP16 ( a-addr --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: +LOOP16 ( a-addr --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (+LOOP) HERE - , RESOLVE-LEAVE 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

: ?DO16 ( --- a-addr )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (?DO) 'LEAVE @ HERE 'LEAVE ! 0 , HERE ; IMMEDIATE

: LEAVE16 ( --- )
  OPERAND-ALIGN POSTPONE (LEAVE) HERE 'LEAVE @ , 'LEAVE ! ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- DOES> -----------------------------------------------------------
\ A DOES> word's body is [DODOES][call token][pad][PFA], where the cell
\ version wrote a single relative-call cell. CREATE16 has already laid
\ down [DOVAR][pad], so there is exactly room to overwrite.

: !CALL ( a-addr target --- )
  START @ - CPT-SHIFT RSHIFT 256 + SWAP !W ;

: (;CODE)16 ( --- )
\ Runtime for DOES>: point the last created word at the code following
\ the call to this word, and end the defining word - the R> discards its
\ return address, exactly as the cell version does.
  R> ALIGNED LAST @ NAME>
  DODOES-OP OVER !W
  2 + SWAP !CALL ;

: DOES>16 ( --- )
\ The tail must be an aligned call target: a call is a SCALED offset, so
\ an unaligned tail address loses its low bits and jumps to the wrong
\ place. The pad is dead code - (;CODE) never returns here.
  POSTPONE (;CODE) ALIGN POSTPONE R> ; IMMEDIATE

\ ---- debugging aid --------------------------------------------------
: TOKENS. ( a-addr n --- )
\ Print n tokens from a-addr. Compiled by the CELL compiler and
\ translated, so it still works when the runtime compiler does not.
  0 DO DUP I 2 * + W@ . LOOP DROP CR ;
